Following rapid expansion, ZBazar has recently acquired two local grocery supply chains, FreshCo and QuickMart, to bolster its regional product inventory. However, the engineering team faces a major synchronization crisis: both acquired companies manage their inventory systems using completely incompatible, proprietary code bases that cannot be modified.FreshCo Systems: Tracks items using a legacy array-based cursor model. To view stock, you must instantiate their custom system and call getAvailableUnits() and getBatchExpirationDate().
2. QuickMart Systems: Relies on a legacy map-based payload. They use entirely different naming conventions, exposing methods like retrieveStockQuantity() and fetchShelfLifeRemainingDays().
Meanwhile, ZBazar’s modern core platform expects all stock entities to conform to a standard, uniform interface called InventoryComponent.To make matters more challenging, ZBazar organizes its warehouses hierarchically. A National Distribution Hub contains multiple Regional Warehouses, which contain Section Aisle Blocks, which ultimately hold individual inventory items or even whole third-party legacy storage containers.
Technical RequirementsInterface Unification (Adapter Pattern): Create structural Object Adapters for both FreshCo and QuickMart systems so that their data can be requested using ZBazar's standard checkStock() and getExpiryStatus() methods without modifying the legacy source classes.
Tree Hierarchy Aggregation (Composite Pattern): Design a unified structural tree where a manager can query stock levels or check expiry profiles on a single item, an adapted legacy container, or an entire multi-tier warehouse network using a single unified method call.